:"code","source":["def create_data_lists(data, y_column):\n","    \"\"\"Split the data into folds and create lists.\n","\n","    Keyword arguments:\n","    data -- a dataset\n","    y_column -- the column from the dataset that contains the\n","        dependent variable\n","\n","    Return values:\n","    X_famd_df_train_list -- a list of X training data sets\n","    X_famd_df_test_list -- a list of X test data sets\n","    y_train_list -- a list of y training data sets\n","    y_test_list -- a list of y test data sets\n","    \"\"\"\n","    # Establish dependent and independent variables.\n","    X = data.drop(columns=y_column, axis=1)\n","    y = data[y_column]\n","\n","    # Establish lists for X and y training and test data lists.\n","    X_famd_df_train_list, X_famd_df_test_list = [], []\n","    y_train_list, y_test_list = [], []\n","\n","    # Cut the data into a reasonable number of discrete bins to ensure\n","    # that StratifiedKFold takes data from across the range of the\n","    # dependent variable.\n","    y_binned = pd.cut(x=y, bins=5, labels=False, include_lowest=True)\n","\n","    # Initialize StratifiedKFold with a reasonable number of splits.\n","    skf = StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=1)\n","\n","    # Iterate through the folds.\n","    for train_index, test_index in skf.split(X=X, y=y_binned):\n","        # Create the train and test data.\n","        X_train = X.iloc[train_index]\n","        X_test = X.iloc[test_index]\n","        y_train = y.iloc[train_index].reset_index(drop=True)\n","        y_test = y.iloc[test_index].reset_index(drop=True)\n","\n","        # Instantiate the FAMD class using an ititial value of\n","        # n_components that captures all the components, fit the model\n","        # to the data, and determine the cumulative variance for each\n","        # component.\n","        famd_initial = prince.FAMD(n_components=20)\n","        famd_initial.fit(X_train)\n","        variance_array = famd_initial.cumulative_percentage_of_variance_\n","\n","        # Determine final number of components based on how many are\n","        # needed to explain the desired percentage of the variance.\n","        final_n_components = np.where(\n","            variance_array > VARIANCE_PERCENTAGE\n","            )[0].min() + 1\n","\n","        # Instantiate FAMD with the final number of components.\n","        famd_final = prince.FAMD(\n","            n_components=final_n_components, handle_unknown='ignore'\n","            )\n","\n","        # Fit the final model to the X training and test data, and add\n","        # constants to each.\n","        X_famd_df_train = famd_final.fit_transform(X_train)\n","        X_famd_df_train = X_famd_df_train.reset_index(drop=True)\n","        X_famd_df_train = sm.add_constant(data=X_famd_df_train)\n","\n","        X_famd_df_test = famd_final.transform(X_test)\n","        X_famd_df_test = X_famd_df_test.reset_index(drop=True)\n","        X_famd_df_test = sm.add_constant(data=X_famd_df_test)\n","\n","        # Save the X and y training and test data in the four lists\n","        # created above.\n","        X_famd_df_train_list.append(X_famd_df_train)\n","        X_famd_df_test_list.append(X_famd_df_test)\n","        y_train_list.append(y_train)\n","        y_test_list.append(y_test)\n","\n","    # Return the four lists which now hold data from all folds.\n","    return X_famd_df_train_list, X_famd_df_test_list, y_train_list, y_test_list"],"metadata":{"id":"EiSh74iso0iI","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744337660,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":1,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}}},"execution_count":9,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["The following two cells call the create_data_lists function on the data. The first set of lists will be used for the original data, and the second is a set of copies that will later have influential points removed."],"metadata":{"id":"6CB1PNhcs9V9"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["(X_famd_df_train_list_original, X_famd_df_test_list_original,\n","    y_train_list_original, y_test_list_original) = (\n","        create_data_lists(data=Houses, y_column='price')\n","        )"],"metadata":{"id":"d-K3zRG_T6BU","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744345356,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":7694,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}}},"execution_count":10,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["(X_famd_df_train_list_reduced, X_famd_df_test_list_reduced,\n","    y_train_list_reduced, y_test_list_reduced) = (\n","        create_data_lists(data=Houses, y_column='price')\n","        )"],"metadata":{"id":"c9rMYrEut-u4","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744353939,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":8585,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}}},"execution_count":11,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### The following cells use a histogram with a reasonable number of bins and a qq plot to determine the distribution of the data so that the appropriate GLM can be used."],"metadata":{"id":"iYqh_AyTHug2"}},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":12,"metadata":{"id":"SIqOg5A09g55","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744354255,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":310,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}},"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":430},"outputId":"85f86d31-2f5b-4a61-fdb4-afb26f460930"},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"source":["plt.hist(x=Houses['price'], bins=40)\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["The dependent variable is continuous and limited to values greater than zero, suggesting a gamma or inverse gaussian distribution."],"metadata":{"id":"L2Eo85KqI40d"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["fig = sm.qqplot(data=Houses['price'], dist=invgauss, fit=True, line='q')\n","plt.title(label=\"QQ Plot\")\n","plt.show()"],"metadata":{"id":"B1-au-55SlUV","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744354564,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":307,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}},"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":472},"outputId":"285feb80-cba1-4002-8a90-85dd535357a9"},"execution_count":13,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"JALmhqrj7hkb"},"source":["The qq plot displays the points close to the reference line when testing for an inverse gaussian distribution, suggesting that this distribution is most approriate."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### The function in this cell creates a model for each fold based on the datasets created previously."],"metadata":{"id":"D0zEnAxRJjr0"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["def create_model_lists(X_famd_df_train_list, y_train_list, remove_influential):\n","    \"\"\"Create a GLM model list from the training data lists.\n","\n","    Keyword arguments:\n","    X_famd_df_train_list -- a list of X training data sets\n","    y_train_list -- a list of y training data sets\n","    remove_influential -- a boolean value indicating whether or not\n","        influential points should be removed\n","\n","    Return values:\n","    model_list -- a list of GLM models\n","    X_famd_df_train_list -- the provided list of X training data\n","        sets, potentially with influential points removed\n","    y_train_list -- the provided list of y training data sets,\n","        potnetially with influential points removed\n","    \"\"\"\n","    # Establish the model list.\n","    model_list = []\n","\n","    # Iterate through the X train list.\n","    for i in range(len(X_famd_df_train_list)):\n","        # Create the model.\n","        model_glm = sm.GLM(\n","            endog=y_train_list[i], exog=X_famd_df_train_list[i],\n","            family=sm.families.InverseGaussian(\n","                link=sm.families.links.Log()\n","                )\n","            ).fit(cov_type='HC3')\n","\n","        # Remove influential points if the remove_influential parameter\n","        # is set to True.\n","        if remove_influential:\n","            # Determine the influential point indices based on Cook's\n","            # distance with 4 over the training data's length as the\n","            # threshold.\n","            influence = model_glm.get_influence()\n","            cooks_distance = influence.cooks_distance[0]\n","            influential_threshold = 4 / len(y_train_list[i])\n","            indices = np.where(cooks_distance > influential_threshold)[0]\n","\n","            # Remove the influential points from the X and y training\n","            # data.\n","            X_famd_df_train_list[i] = X_famd_df_train_list[i].drop(\n","                index=indices\n","                )\n","            y_train_list[i] = y_train_list[i].drop(\n","                index=indices\n","                )\n","\n","            # Refit the model with the reduced training data.\n","            model_glm = sm.GLM(\n","                endog=y_train_list[i], exog=X_famd_df_train_list[i],\n","                family=sm.families.InverseGaussian(\n","                    link=sm.families.links.Log()\n","                    )\n","                ).fit(cov_type='HC3')\n","\n","        # Save the model in the list created above.\n","        model_list.append(model_glm)\n","\n","    # Return the model and training data lists.\n","    return model_list, X_famd_df_train_list, y_train_list"],"metadata":{"id":"WhZ2JMKvgizJ","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744354585,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":20,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}}},"execution_count":14,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["The next two cells call the create_model_list function on the two sets of training data created earlier. One sets the remove_influential parameter to False and the other to True, creating lists with and without influential points."],"metadata":{"id":"3ZxWEvCsMbdU"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["model_list_original, X_famd_df_train_list_original, y_train_list_original = (\n","    create_model_lists(\n","        X_famd_df_train_list=X_famd_df_train_list_original,\n","        y_train_list=y_train_list_original, remove_influential=False\n","        )\n","    )"],"metadata":{"id":"sb8gqnjziLdQ","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744354682,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":97,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}}},"execution_count":15,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["model_list_reduced, X_famd_df_train_list_reduced, y_train_list_reduced = (\n","    create_model_lists(\n","        X_famd_df_train_list=X_famd_df_train_list_reduced,\n","        y_train_list=y_train_list_reduced, remove_influential=True\n","        )\n","    )"],"metadata":{"id":"YBQeMpRPiSWa","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744355062,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":374,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}}},"execution_count":16,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["It's worth pointing out that the two sets of test data (X_famd_df_test_list and X_famd_df_test_list_reduced as well as y_test_list and y_test_list_reduced) are each identical. While this is somewhat redundant, the copies are used to make the project more consistent and easier to follow."],"metadata":{"id":"JDnpZ0gWYflh"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["### Multicollinearity was addressed with FAMD and sample size was shown to be sufficient; this cell creates a function for examining the reamaining model assumptions through qq plots and scatter plots."],"metadata":{"id":"acsL-hGENOPT"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["def assumption_plots(model_list, X_famd_df_list, y_list, train):\n","    \"\"\"Create an array of plots to examine the model assumptions.\n","\n","    Keyword arguments:\n","    model_list -- a list of GLM models\n","    X_famd_df_list -- a list of X training data sets\n","    y_list -- a list of y training data sets\n","    train -- a boolean value indicating whether or not the data is\n","        training data\n","    \"\"\"\n","    # Establish subplots so that both plots for each fold can be\n","    # displayed.\n","    fig, axes = plt.subplots(nrows=2, ncols=len(model_list), figsize=(25, 10))\n","\n","    # Iterate through the model list.\n","    for i in range(len(model_list)):\n","        # Acquire quantile residuals for each model.\n","        mu_fit, loc_fit, scale_fit = invgauss.fit(data=y_list[i])\n","        cdf_values = invgauss.cdf(\n","            x=y_list[i], mu=mu_fit, scale=scale_fit, loc=loc_fit\n","            )\n","        quantile_residuals = norm.ppf(q=cdf_values)\n","\n","        # Acquire the prediction values for each model.\n","        predictions = model_list[i].predict(X_famd_df_list[i])\n","\n","        # Acquire the pearson residuals for each model.\n","        pearson_residuals = (y_list[i]-predictions)**2 / predictions**3\n","\n","        # Acqurie the dispersion parameter estimate for each model.\n","        residual_df = len(y_list[i]) - X_famd_df_list[i].shape[1]\n","        dispersion = np.sum(a=pearson_residuals) / residual_df\n","\n","        # Acquire the deviance residuals for each model.\n","        deviance_residuals = (\n","            np.sign(y_list[i]-predictions)\n","            * np.sqrt((y_list[i]-predictions)**2/(y_list[i]*predictions**2))\n","            )\n","\n","        # Acqurie standardized deviance residuals, with the formula\n","        # dependent on whether training or test data is used.\n","        if train:\n","            influence = model_list[i].get_influence()\n","            leverage = influence.hat_matrix_diag\n","            standardized_residuals = (\n","                deviance_residuals / np.sqrt(dispersion*(1-leverage))\n","                )\n","        else:\n","            standardized_residuals = deviance_residuals / np.sqrt(dispersion)\n","\n","        # Check that the correct model is used with a qq plot (the\n","        # quanitle residuals should be normally distributed), an\n","        # Anderson Darling test, and a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test.\n","        sm.qqplot(\n","            data=np.sort(quantile_residuals), dist=norm, fit=True, line='45',\n","            ax=axes[0, i]\n","            )\n","        ad_test_inv_gauss = diagnostic.normal_ad(x=quantile_residuals)[1]\n","        ks_test_inv_gauss = diagnostic.kstest_normal(x=quantile_residuals)[1]\n","\n","        # Display the plots and test information.\n","        axes[0, i].set_title(label='Quantile Residuals QQ Plot ' + str(i + 1))\n","        axes[0, i].text(\n","            x=0.5, y=-0.12,\n","            s='Anderson-Darling p-value:   ' + str(ad_test_inv_gauss),\n","            transform=axes[0, i].transAxes, ha='center', va='top'\n","            )\n","        axes[0, i].text(\n","            x=0.5, y=-0.17,\n","            s='Kolmogorov-Smirnov p-value: '  + str(ks_test_inv_gauss),\n","            transform=axes[0, i].transAxes, ha='center', va='top'\n","            )\n","\n","        # Create confidence bands for the qq plots using a reasonable\n","        # significance level.\n","        n = len(y_list[i])\n","        lower_band = norm.ppf(\n","            q=beta.ppf(\n","                q=SIGNIFICANCE_LEVEL / 2, a=np.arange(start=1, stop=n + 1),\n","                b=np.arange(start=n, stop=0, step=-1)\n","                )\n","            )\n","        upper_band = norm.ppf(\n","            q=beta.ppf(\n","                q=1 - SIGNIFICANCE_LEVEL/2, a=np.arange(start=1, stop=n + 1),\n","                b=np.arange(start=n, stop=0, step=-1)\n","                )\n","            )\n","        axes[0, i].fill_between(\n","            x=np.sort(norm.ppf(np.linspace(start=0, stop=1, num=n))),\n","            y1=lower_band, y2=upper_band, color='lightblue', alpha=0.9\n","            )\n","\n","        # Check that the variance function is correct (variance should\n","        # be proportional to the cube of the mean), the observations\n","        # are independent, and there is a linear relationship between\n","        # the predictors and the link function of the mean of the\n","        # response. This is done using a scatter plot and a White's\n","        # test. If all are these assumptions are true, the standardized\n","        # deviance residuals plotted against the fitted values on the\n","        # link scale should be a random scatter.\n","        axes[1, i].scatter(x=predictions, y=standardized_residuals)\n","        axes[1, i].axhline(y=0, color='r', linestyle='-')\n","        axes[1, i].set_xscale(value='log')\n","        axes[1, i].set_title(\n","            label='Standardized Residuals Scatter Plot ' + str(i + 1)\n","            )\n","\n","        # Display the plots and test information.\n","        white_test_standardized = diagnostic.het_white(\n","            resid=standardized_residuals, exog=X_famd_df_list[i]\n","            )[1]\n","        axes[1, i].text(\n","            x=0.5, y=-0.1,\n","            s=\"White's p-value: \" + str(white_test_standardized),\n","            transform=axes[1, i].transAxes, ha='center', va='top'\n","            )\n","\n","    # Display the plots.\n","    plt.tight_layout()\n","    plt.show()"],"metadata":{"id":"2U_UQ1ZgiZ-F","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744355135,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":64,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}}},"execution_count":17,"outputs":[]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["The next two cells run the assumption_plots function on the training data for the original and reduced data. It's important to note that qq plots tend to display deviation from the given line in the tails. Also, with many data points, even small deviations from normality or homoscedasticity can lead to low p-values in statistical tests. Fortunately, when the goal of a regression project is prediction, significant p-values (those below the 0.05 threshold) aren't extremely concerning."],"metadata":{"id":"92WjM0BhPzYA"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["assumption_plots(\n","    model_list=model_list_original,\n","    X_famd_df_list=X_famd_df_train_list_original,\n","    y_list=y_train_list_original, train=True\n","    )"],"metadata":{"id":"vQN6Zzr6UTNB","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744365186,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":10050,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}},"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":477},"outputId":"0d3aedbf-3735-49dc-8d22-77d9d730f1b0"},"execution_count":18,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 2500x1000 with 10 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["assumption_plots(\n","    model_list=model_list_reduced,\n","    X_famd_df_list=X_famd_df_train_list_reduced,\n","    y_list=y_train_list_reduced, train=True\n","    )"],"metadata":{"id":"0X1sfZmHXemY","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1767744369444,"user_tz":360,"elapsed":4250,"user":{"displayName":"Sean Graham","userId":"14400275736943667243"}},"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":477},"outputId":"dbe8490a-f911-4af3-e7bc-6fb5ac8bf825"},"execution_count":19,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 2500x1000 with 10 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["Based on the qq plots and scatter plots of the training data, all the assumptions seem to be satisfied (even if some p-values are signfificant). The next two cells will run the function on the two sets of test data for additional confirmation that the assumptions are met. It should be noted that since the original and reduced versions of the X and y test data are the same, the plots and statistical test values are